WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.699
Speaker 1: Why don't we go next to.

00:00:03.699 --> 00:00:05.980
Oh, I don't know.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.130
Why don't we try George from Chicago.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.830
George, you're on the air.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:12.980
That's my audio.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.150
Your audio is maybe about a six and a half,
seven, not bad.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:19.310
Speaker 2: Oh, all right, well, David.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:24.539
Speaker 1: Oh, I'm sorry, your audio just
dropped to a five.

00:00:24.539 --> 00:00:27.390
Speaker 2: Oh, oh, geez, I'm sorry.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:29.350
I'll try to get real quick, OK?

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:30.529
Yeah, please.

00:00:30.529 --> 00:00:38.719
OK, David, I heard an article in the comments
that talked about rising or some of the stimulus

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:41.960
preventing people from going into the workforce.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.640
Do you think it's a little bit too soon to
say whether or not that's a good call to do

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:47.640
that story yet?

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.999
Speaker 1: Yeah, so I did a segment about
this earlier this week and spent about, you

00:00:50.999 --> 00:00:52.821
know, 10 or 15 minutes talking about it.

00:00:52.821 --> 00:00:56.170
Here's my view on this, because I think this
is really important.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:01:00.559
It's a very popular right wing argument right
now to say because of the enhanced unemployment,

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:02.929
people are just raking in money by not working.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:06.350
And so this is causing all these restaurants
to be unable to hire people.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:11.710
And here's the bottom line, enhanced unemployment
benefits.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:17.370
And and I think it's September 6th, but it's
I believe sometime in September, once that

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:23.100
happens, all sorts of people that right now
might be choosing not to go back to work because

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.510
if they go back to work, they might make the
same or a little less or whatever because

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.350
of the enhanced unemployment.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.870
They're going to need to go and get get jobs.

00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:31.480
They're just going to need to go and do it.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.210
Unemployment's going to end.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:38.340
And it's very easy to just look at a social
welfare program and point to what you would

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.930
consider to be the free riders or people taking
advantage of it, making more off of the program

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.000
than from work.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.161
But that's going to come to an end.

00:01:45.161 --> 00:01:50.730
And hence, unemployment was hugely beneficial
to the country at a macroeconomic level.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:55.050
And in the next three to four months, I believe
this is going to resolve itself.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.960
George's people are going to need jobs.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.080
There's going to be no more unemployment,
and some businesses will have to pay more.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.221
And there's no question about it.

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:05.390
And there is an argument to be made in some
states.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:08.800
If you can't get people to come and work for
you, maybe you're not paying a high enough

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:09.800
wage.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.090
It is true that dropping enhanced unemployment
on top of state unemployment distorts the

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.090
labor market.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.810
That's absolutely undeniable.

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:23.390
The question is, is it exposing that the wages
are too low?

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.430
In some cases, the answer is yes.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.080
And there's not a one size fits all answer
to that.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.900
Different states have different minimum wages,
but I believe this will be resolved by October

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:32.900
15th.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:33.900
Speaker 2: OK.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:34.900
OK, thank you.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.170
I just thought that article was a little bit
too soon and lampooned the stimulus a little

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:39.170
bit too much.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:40.170
I agree with you.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.249
Just a real quick question.

00:02:41.249 --> 00:02:42.249
Yeah.

00:02:42.249 --> 00:02:46.840
I'm going to be through whether you call it
fortunate or unfortunate circumstances, I'd

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.319
say fortunate in Boston, Massachusetts, next
week.

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:51.790
Anything in particular you think I should
see?

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.900
Speaker 1: Have you been to the city before?

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.659
Speaker 2: No, sir.

00:02:55.659 --> 00:02:59.879
Speaker 1: OK, when when I would you know,
when people when I was in Boston and people

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:05.000
would come visit me if they had never been
before, I would sort of give them a tour that

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:11.980
includes the popular tourist, the area of
the Boston Common Area, a Newbury Street along

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:14.599
the river between Boston and Cambridge.

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:19.810
You definitely want to go over to Cambridge,
check out Harvard Yard, check out Amitay,

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:27.709
check out that area, but then also go down
to Southie and Dorchester, go to Castle Island,

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:31.980
go to some of these other, you know, new hip
neighborhoods.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:32.980
What else?

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:33.980
Yeah, I don't know.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.310
I mean, that's probably already enough for
a weekend.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.310
Speaker 2: Yeah.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.310
Yeah.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:38.310
Thank you.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:39.310
All right.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.310
Speaker 1: Have a go on, David.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:41.310
All right, George.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.310
Absolutely.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:43.310
A perfect phone call.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:44.310
Really can't be.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:45.310
We're off to a great start today.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:04:44.150
Let's see if we can keep it going.

